  {
    "episode_id": 91315,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91315.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "So, first of all, we had a hearing week before last, where we had Cleeta Mitchell, who's one of the lawyers, and worked in around Trump World, and she was great, and wonderful and testified. We also had the Secretary of State of Florida, who was fantastic. We made clear that the roles that have been cleared off in Texas, 6,500 non-citizens have been pulled off, of whom at least 2,000 had been there voting. We had 6,300 in Virginia, with a very large number who had in fact voted. There was a large number in Ohio, the number you referenced in Oregon, there's a long list of states where we've got data that demonstrates that non-citizens were in fact on the voting holes, but here's the real trick. We don't actually know. The Secretary of State of Florida testified to what I already knew. which is that the current federal databases are not really made fully available to states unless they fight to get access to them. And even then, the database they get access to is actually ironically or similarly called the Save Database. They access it and they don't get what they're supposed to get. They need better data. The Save Act would give them access to all the data for citizen data to be able to make determinations of whether they're in fact citizens. So we don't even know how many non-citizens are voting because we can't check it all. Arizona passed a law that said that you must have documentary proof of citizenship to register. But the courts came in and said, you can't enforce that on federal elections. So they have a bibricated system.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
